//
// REPRO: interpolating a struct that has a `hash<…>` field SIGSEGVs
// the interpreter in OpFormatDatabase.
//
// Run:  loft --interpret loft_repros/format_struct_with_hash_field.loft
//
// ── Trigger ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `"{s}"` where `s` is a struct with a hash-typed field, and the hash
// has at least one entry.  A plain struct formats fine; so does the
// hash's own element.  It is the parent struct that crashes.
//
// ── Observed (loft 2026.8.0, interpreter) ───────────────────────
//
//   === loft crash (loft) SIGSEGV caught ===
//     last op:  OpFormatDatabase (op=145)
//
// ── Expected ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Either a formatted value (`{cells: {…}, n: 1}`, the way `:j` and
// `{x:#}` render other records) or a clean compile-time refusal —
// "a struct with a hash field cannot be formatted" would be a
// perfectly good answer.  A segfault is not.
//
// ── Why it matters more than it looks ───────────────────────────
//
// The crash is in an ASSERTION MESSAGE.  Hit by dryopea while
// writing the flow-field tests: `assert(a > b, "sizes differ: {f1}
// vs {f2}")` where the two are `FlowField` records.  So the failure
// arrives while a test is already failing, replaces the diagnostic
// that would have explained it with a segfault, and points at the
// wrong line — the crash report's "nearest span" was 3 lines off.
//
// A message only evaluated on failure is exactly where a crash is
// most expensive and least likely to be caught by the suite.

struct Cell  { q: integer, r: integer, v: integer }
struct Field { cells: hash<Cell[q, r]>, n: integer }

fn main() {
    r_f = Field { cells: [], n: 0 };
    r_f.cells[1, 2] = Cell { q: 1, r: 2, v: 7 };
    r_f.n = 1;

    // These are all fine:
    r_one = r_f.cells[1, 2] ?? Cell {};
    println("the element formats: {r_one}");
    println("a plain field formats: {r_f.n}");

    // This segfaults:
    println("the parent struct: {r_f}");
    println("NOT REACHED");
}
